<Layout
  title="MEMoPAD Co-Design with People Living with Anxiety Disorders"
  description="How MEMoPAD was co-designed over seven phases with people living with anxiety disorders, carers and mental health clinicians: methods and lessons."
  ogType="article"
  publishedTime={published}
  breadcrumbName="Co-Design"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" is:inline set:html={JSON.stringify(articleSchema)} />

  <article class="container codesign">
    <h1>{title}</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      MEMoPAD was not designed <em>for</em> people living with anxiety disorders; it was designed <em>with</em> them.
      Over seven phases, patients, unpaid carers and mental health clinicians have shaped what the tool does, how it looks
      and how it speaks to the person wearing it. This page explains how we did it, what came out of it, and what we
      learned about co-design with this population.
    </p>
    <p class="event-note">
      This page accompanies our talk and poster at the
      <a href="https://www.mentalhealthplatform.ac.uk/event/mhp-research-summit-2026" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Mental Health Platform Research Summit</a>
      (Sheffield, 8 October 2026).
    </p>

    <h2>Why co-design?</h2>
    <p>
      Digital mental health tools often show promise in trials but lose their users in daily life: attrition has been called
      the “Achilles heel” of digital therapeutics <a href="#ref-1" class="cite">[1]</a>. Anxiety disorders add their own
      barriers. Avoidance, cognitive overload and fear of judgement are among the symptoms these tools aim to address, and
      they may also work against sustained use of the tool itself <a href="#ref-3" class="cite">[3]</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Co-design is one response: the people who will live with a technology help decide what it should be. We adapted the
      co-design framework of Noorbergen and colleagues <a href="#ref-2" class="cite">[2]</a>, from understanding the
      problem to testing in daily life, and ran it with three groups:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Patients</strong>: people living with anxiety disorders. We use “patients” because it was the term our participants preferred in a terminology survey.</li>
      <li><strong>Unpaid carers</strong>: the family members and friends who support them.</li>
      <li><strong>Mental health clinicians</strong>, from the NHS and private practice.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="phases">Seven phases at a glance</h2>
    <div class="table-wrapper" role="region" aria-labelledby="phases-caption" tabindex="0">
      <table>
        <caption id="phases-caption">
          MEMoPAD co-design phases. Numbers are per phase: many people took part in more than one phase, so they should not be added up.
        </caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Phase</th>
            <th scope="col">Question</th>
            <th scope="col">Main tools</th>
            <th scope="col">Who took part</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {PHASES.map((p) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">
                <span class="phase-id">{p.id}</span> {p.name}
                <span class="phase-date">{p.status === 'ongoing' ? `From ${monthYear(p.date)}, under way` : `Completed ${monthYear(p.date)}`}</span>
              </th>
              <td data-label="Question">{p.question}</td>
              <td data-label="Main tools">{p.tools}</td>
              <td data-label="Who took part">{p.breakdown}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>How each phase worked</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>A format for each group.</strong> In Phases I–IV, held online as most participants preferred, patients met in focus groups of up to six people, lasting about two hours. Carers and clinicians took part in separate one-to-one online interviews of around 30 minutes, to limit power imbalances and fit busy diaries.</li>
      <li><strong>Building on the last phase.</strong> Every phase opened by presenting what the previous one had found, so all three groups worked from the same evidence.</li>
      <li><strong>Tools matched to the question.</strong> A survey to map needs, storyboards to generate ideas, mock-ups to shape the interface, a click-through demo to test it, then the working prototype in the lab and in daily life.</li>
      <li><strong>Analysis.</strong> Thematic analysis (Phases I–II) and affinity diagrams (Phases III–V), compared across the three groups, then a roundtable with the interdisciplinary supervisory team.</li>
      <li><strong>Staying in touch.</strong> A <a href="/newsletter/">monthly newsletter</a> and this website kept participants informed between phases.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What participants shaped</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Three scenarios (Phase I).</strong> Participants chose where MEMoPAD should help: (A) supporting clinical conversations, which are short and rely on recall; (B) breaking anxiety loops in the moment; and (C) helping people seek help at the right time.</li>
      <li><strong>Watch plus phone (Phase II).</strong> A smartwatch collects data continuously and a paired phone app is where people see it and add to it. Personalisation came out as essential, and body data only made sense alongside the person’s own notes and context.</li>
      <li><strong>Emotions, not pathology (across phases).</strong> Participants rejected a tool that monitors anxiety. They wanted one that reflects their whole emotional life, pleasant moments included. This led to a four-colour vocabulary, now called <strong>Emotion Hues</strong>: Green (calm or relaxed), Yellow (nervous or excited), Red (panicky or angry) and Blue (tired or low).</li>
      <li><strong>Two audiences, one dataset (Phases III–IV).</strong> A web dashboard shows clinicians what a patient chooses to share, using the same views the patient sees. Carers asked for practical and emotional support without it becoming surveillance.</li>
      <li><strong>A check with fresh eyes (Phase V).</strong> 14 of the 16 lab participants were new to MEMoPAD, yet they valued the features that earlier groups had prioritised.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Five lessons for co-design with people living with anxiety disorders</h2>
    <ol class="lessons">
      <li><strong>Real-time group tools can backfire.</strong> Collaborative digital whiteboards added pressure and risked raising anxiety in the session itself. Lower-pressure, asynchronous and analogue options, such as sending a photo of a hand-annotated mock-up over WhatsApp, were more inclusive.</li>
      <li><strong>Invite carers from the start.</strong> Patients asked for it in our first consultations. Anxiety disorders are lived within a network of family, friends and services, and designing for an isolated user misses how they are managed day to day.</li>
      <li><strong>Build low-touch routes for clinicians.</strong> Clinician involvement fell over the phases. Short online interviews and asynchronous feedback suit time-poor experts better than long focus groups.</li>
      <li><strong>Frame around emotions, not disorders.</strong> Tracking the full emotional spectrum, rather than monitoring pathology, may matter for sustained engagement as much as any single feature.</li>
      <li><strong>Close the loop every time.</strong> Opening each phase with the last one’s findings, and sharing progress monthly, let participants see what their input had changed.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Where we are now</h2>
    <p>
      Phase VI is under way: 10 participants in Bristol are using the working watch and phone app in their daily lives for
      up to five weeks. Its results will be reported in the PhD thesis and in forthcoming papers. To see what the prototype
      measures, and what it deliberately does not claim, read
      <a href="/research/affective-computing/">What can a smartwatch tell us about emotions?</a>
    </p>
    <div class="button-group cta">
      <a href="/take-part/" class="button">Take part: clinicians and carers</a>
      <a href="/newsletter/" class="button button-secondary">Follow the project</a>
    </div>

    <h2>Our papers on co-design</h2>
    <ul class="papers">
      {codesignPapers.map((p) => (
        <li><a href={`/research/publications/${p.slug}/`}>{p.title}</a> <span>({p.venueShort}, {p.year})</span></li>
